  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nIsolated to scattered thunderstorms are possible this evening \ninto tonight, mostly north of Highway 30 and west of Highway 183\nas the right-entrance of a mid-level jet streak exits northeast\nNE. The likeliest zone of storm activity looks to scrape right \nalong a stretch of area from Broken Bow to Ord between 6 PM this\nevening and 12 AM Wednesday morning. Confidence is high that \ncoverage will be spotty in nature as synoptic forcing for ascent\nwill be limited. A strong to severe thunderstorm capable of \nwind gusts to 60 mph and hail up to 1\" in diameter will be \npossible this evening, with a few 2\"+ stones possible for our \nnorthernmost locations where shear most overlaps with buoyancy. \nSPC has also included a 2% tornado risk for these areas where 12z \nHREF probabilities suggest marginal overlap in STP and high cell \nreflectivities. The heaviest storm cells look to be capable of \nproducing precip rates of 0.25\"-0.5\" per hour (~30% chances to \nsee these rates) owing to HRRR- and RRFS-progged PWATs of ~1.5\" \ncoupled with MLCAPE of 2600-3100 J/kg. \n\nBeyond tonight, very low chances (<10%) of isolated precip \ncontinue each day given our sandwiched position between the \nwestern CONUS trough and the persistent east-central ridge. \nDespite this, the overwhelming majority of the CWA is favored to\nremain dry, and any additional rainfall will be minuscule in \namounts. The next \"reasonable\" precip chances look to arrive \nFriday night into Saturday (~20% chances), and again Saturday \nnight into Sunday (20-30% chances). \n\nThose of us who enjoy cool fall weather will have to wait. A \nhot start to September is expected to continue as an upper-level\nridge and surface high pressure slowly meanders across the \neast- central CONUS. Temperatures in the 90s F to low 100s F \nwill likely stick around as we get into Labor Day weekend. Our \nsix KS counties are the likeliest to see temps hit 100 F over \nthe next few days (12z HREF probabilities ~50-70%) with overnight\nlows bottoming out in the mid 70s F. Despite modest dewpoints, \nlatest NBM guidance indicates heat indices will approach and may \nexceed 105 F Friday afternoon into the weekend for the \nsoutheastern border counties of our CWA. The best chances for any \novernight relief will be our far northwestern counties bordering \nNWS North Platte's CWA, where temperatures can dip to the low-to-\nmid 60s F (~60% chances of lows <65 F). The next \"cool down\" looks\nto arrive late this weekend to bring in Labor Day, but this \nregrettably doesn't look to be anything more than a few degrees \nrelief. CPC continues to foresee warmer-than-average temperatures \nthrough the next two weeks. \n\nAs far as winds go, a diffuse frontal boundary will gradually \nretreat northwards through the rest of Tuesday, shifting winds \nnorth of Highway 30 from northerly to southerly as it passes. \nThis boundary may re-shift southward overnight into Wednesday \nand repeat the process tomorrow morning. South of Highway 30, \ngenerally southerly flow will prevail into Labor Day weekend. \nWind speeds will stay mostly steady from 5 - 15 mph throughout \nthe forecast period, with the exception of a few pockets of 10 -\n20 mph winds in KS where daytime heating and mixing will boost \nwinds in the afternoon and evening hours each day. A few gusts \ncan reach as high as 25-30 mph south of I-80.",
